\section{Choose the boundary of the first system}

Start with a reader, a printer, a small lexical evaluator, and a REPL with a persistent top-level environment. Support booleans, exact integers within a documented range, symbols, and pairs; provide \texttt{quote}, \texttt{if}, \texttt{lambda}, application, and enough definition and assignment to build a library. Publish what is absent. A subset with bounded integers, no hygienic expander, or no first-class continuations is still useful, but not a complete implementation of a Scheme report. Nils M.\ Holm's \emph{Scheme 9 from Empty Space} demonstrates a later point on this path: a broad R5RS subset with a reader, evaluator, collector, and number machinery that can be inspected as one system.

The reader recognizes external representations of data. It rewrites \verb|'x| to the datum \verb|(quote x)|, but it does not decide whether an unquoted pair is a legal application. It must distinguish end-of-input from the empty list and a truncated list from a clean end-of-input. Put well-formedness checks on core expressions at the evaluator's entrance: the reader should accept \verb|(a . b)| as data even when the evaluator rejects it as an application. A reader--printer round trip tests the data boundary; it need not preserve whitespace or the chosen quotation shorthand.

\section{Environments and the evaluator loop}

Paul R.\ Wilson's \emph{An Introduction to Scheme and its Implementation} explains lexical environments as a sequence of binding contours. Implement a contour as a frame of locations plus a parent pointer. A closure contains its formals, body, and definition environment; a call extends that saved environment with fresh argument locations. Lookups in the caller are used to evaluate the operator and operands, not to resolve the callee's free variables. With \texttt{set!}, update the resolved location rather than installing a new entry in the current frame.

Give the evaluator a loop with registers for the current expression and environment. In a conditional, obtain the test value, place the selected expression in the expression register, and iterate. In a closure application, evaluate operator and operands, bind arguments, perform any non-final body expressions, and iterate on the last body expression in the new environment. These tail transitions replace pending work rather than calling the host evaluator recursively. Non-tail work still needs records for saved operands or pending tests. If first-class continuations are added, represent those records explicitly so the evaluator can capture them (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-166}); the host stack is not automatically an adequate implementation of Scheme control. Shriram Krishnamurthi's \emph{Programming Languages: Application and Interpretation} separates desugaring from the interpreter, a useful precedent for keeping derived forms out of this loop.

\section{Trace a closure through collection}

Run this in a single top-level environment. In a minimal evaluator without \texttt{let}, the lambda application makes the otherwise hidden contour explicit:

\begin{verbatim}
(define next
  ((lambda (n)
     (lambda () (begin (set! n (+ n 1)) n)))
   0))
(next)  ; 1
(next)  ; 2
\end{verbatim}

After the outer lambda finishes, its frame remains live through the top-level slot for \texttt{next}, the closure, and the closure's saved environment. Both calls find the same location for \texttt{n}. Force collection after the definition and between calls; additionally force collection while evaluating operands in a call whose earlier operands have already produced heap objects. A local variable in the C evaluator holding such an object is not a root unless registered or visible under the collector's documented root convention. Holm's nodes are indices into separate \texttt{Car}, \texttt{Cdr}, and \texttt{Tag} arrays; the indirection makes the root discipline no less necessary. Conversely, a REPL result should not be rooted forever just because the evaluator used it on one iteration.

\section{Bootstrapping without losing a baseline}

Load list helpers and derived operations from Scheme source after installing the primitives. Run the same library tests once in the minimal interpreter and again if a bytecode engine is introduced. Check the fixed test results and record peak pending control frames for an allocation-free tail loop. Also keep a test for shadowing a primitive with a local procedure; the initial environment is not a keyword table. These tests cover separate layers and make failures local enough to diagnose. The dossier's meta-circular compiler discussion (Chapter~\ref{ch:meta-circular}) takes this layering farther: self-hosting is valuable, but it does not remove the need to identify what the original loader and executable supply.
